<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Terengganu Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1708 CE – present (approx.) · capital Kuala Terengganu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">East-coast Malay sultanate founded by a Johor prince, long a tributary of Siam, and today a state of Malaysia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terengganu-inscription-stone">Terengganu Inscription Stone</a> of the early 14th century shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> there centuries earlier, but the present sultanate was founded in 1708 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zainal-abidin-i-of-terengganu">Tun Zainal Abidin</a>, a son of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate</a>'s Bendahara family, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Zainal Abidin I. Terengganu later sent the bunga mas, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> trees, as tribute to Siam.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans governed a coastal society of fishermen, boat builders, weavers and traders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and gold. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baginda-omar">Baginda Omar</a> in the 19th century strengthened royal power and encouraged crafts such as brocade weaving. Islamic scholarship flourished, and the court resisted foreign advisers longer than most Malay states.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-siamese-treaty-of-1909">Anglo-Siamese Treaty of 1909</a> transferred Terengganu to British protection; it accepted a British adviser in 1919 and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/unfederated-malay-states">Unfederated Malay States</a>. After Japanese and Thai occupation it entered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Federation of Malaya</a> and became a state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a>, its sultan one of the nine rulers who elect the king.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/terengganu-sultanate.jpg" alt="Terengganu Sultanate" width="320" height="405"><figcaption>Image: Eshar · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuanku_Mizan_2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1708 CE – present (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuala Terengganu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zainal Abidin I, Baginda Omar, Mizan Zainal Abidin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Terengganu Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Terengganu, Trengganu, Terengganu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1867&amp;focus=terengganu-sultanate" aria-label="Terengganu Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7539 1978 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Terengganu Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7859 2234L7881 2378M7859 2234L7754 1922M7859 2234L7823 2316"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7881 2378h0M7754 1922h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7859 2234h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7859 2234h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kuala Terengganu. <a href="/atlas?year=1867&amp;focus=terengganu-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
